3. Get Unread Count
Get the number of unread notifications (useful for the bell icon badge).
●	Endpoint: /notifications/unread-count
●	Method: GET
●	Headers: Authorization: Bearer <token>
Response (200 OK)
{
  "count": 5
}

4. Mark Notification as Read
Mark a specific notification as read.
●	Endpoint: /notifications/{id}/read
●	Method: POST
●	Headers: Authorization: Bearer <token>
●	Path Parameters:
○	id: The UUID of the notification.
Response
●	200 OK: Successfully marked as read. (Empty body)
●	404 Not Found: Notification ID not found or does not belong to the user.


Notification Object Structure

This is the standard JSON object used in both the History API and the SSE Stream.

Field	Type	Description
id	UUID (String)	Unique ID of the notification.
userId	String	The user this notification belongs to.
message	String	The text to display to the user.
isRead	Boolean	true if read, false if new.
createdAt	ISO String	Timestamp (e.g., 2023-11-12T10:00:00Z).
linkUrl	String	Relative URL to redirect when clicked.
sourceEventId	String	For tracing/debugging purposes.

React Integration Checklist
1.	[ ] Install @microsoft/fetch-event-source.
2.	[ ] Create a global Context or Hook (useNotification) to manage the SSE connection so it persists across page navigations.
3.	[ ] On App load (if logged in), call GET /notifications/unread-count to set the initial badge number.
4.	[ ] On App load, connect to SSE.
○	When new_notification arrives:
■	Increment the local badge count state.
■	Show a "Toast" or "Snackbar" popup with event.data.message.
■	If the user is currently viewing the Notification List, prepend the new item to the list.

5.	[ ] When user clicks the bell icon:
○	Call GET /notifications?page=0&size=20.
6.	[ ] When user clicks a notification item:
○	Call POST /notifications/{id}/read.
○	Decrement local badge count.
○	Redirect user to linkUrl.
